# 1) Difference Between WAF, NSG, Firewall, and Agents

| Component | Layer | Purpose | Filters Based On |
|------------|-------|----------|------------------|
| WAF | Layer 7 | Protect web applications | HTTP/HTTPS traffic |
| NSG | Layer 3/4 | Control subnet/VM traffic | IP, Port, Protocol |
| Firewall | Layer 3-7 | Centralized network security | IP, Port, URL, FQDN |
| Agent | Host Level | Monitoring and security inside VM | OS/Application activity |

---

# NSG (Network Security Group)

NSG works at:
- Layer 3 (IP)
- Layer 4 (TCP/UDP)

Used for:
- Allow/Deny traffic

Filters:
- Source IP
- Destination IP
- Port
- Protocol

Example:
Allow:
- Port 80
- Port 443

Block:
- Port 22 from Internet

Architecture:

Internet
   |
NSG
   |
Subnet/VM

---

# Firewall

Firewall provides:
- Advanced traffic filtering
- Centralized security

Can inspect:
- Network traffic
- Application traffic

Supports:
- Threat intelligence
- URL filtering
- DNAT/SNAT
- TLS inspection

Example:
Block:
facebook.com

Allow:
company APIs

---

# WAF (Web Application Firewall)

WAF works only for:
- HTTP
- HTTPS

Layer:
- Layer 7

Purpose:
- Protect web applications from attacks

Protects Against:
- SQL Injection
- Cross Site Scripting (XSS)
- Command Injection
- Cookie Poisoning
- Bot attacks

Architecture:

Internet
   |
WAF/Application Gateway
   |
Web Application

---

# Agent

Agent is software installed inside:
- VM
- Server
- Container

Purpose:
- Monitoring
- Security
- Logging
- Threat detection

Examples:
- Monitoring Agent
- Defender Agent
- Antivirus Agent

---

# Real Difference

| Feature | NSG | Firewall | WAF |
|----------|------|----------|------|
| IP Filtering | Yes | Yes | No |
| Port Filtering | Yes | Yes | No |
| HTTP Inspection | No | Limited | Yes |
| SQL Injection Protection | No | Partial | Yes |
| XSS Protection | No | Partial | Yes |
| Layer | 3/4 | 3-7 | 7 |

